3. Fix the bug in this setter:
class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNo;
    void setData(String name, int rollNo) {
        name = name;
        rollNo = rollNo;
    }
}

items.a:
Bug: 'name = name' assigns the parameter to itself — the field 'this.name' is never updated. Same for rollNo. Fix:
void setData(String name, int rollNo) {
    this.name = name;
    this.rollNo = rollNo;
}
The 'this.' prefix distinguishes the instance field from the local parameter of the same name. Without it, Java resolves 'name' to the nearest scope — the parameter — not the field.

public static double multiply(double base, double... multipliers) {
    double result = base;
    for (double m : multipliers) {
        result *= m;
    }
    return result;
}
// Usage:
System.out.println(multiply(2.0));           // 2.0
System.out.println(multiply(2.0, 3.0));      // 6.0
System.out.println(multiply(2.0, 3.0, 4.0)); // 24.0
Note: 'base' is a required fixed parameter, 'multipliers' is varargs (0 or more). If no multipliers are passed, the loop doesn't execute and 'base' is returned unchanged.

items.q:
5. Identify the design problem and refactor:
public void sendNotification(String userId, String message,
                               boolean isEmail, boolean isSMS,
                               boolean isPush, boolean isUrgent) {
    // ...
}

items.a:
Problems: (1) 6 parameters — too many, especially multiple booleans. (2) Call site sendNotification(uid, msg, true, false, true, false) is completely unreadable — what do those booleans mean?
Fix using an Options record:
record NotificationOptions(boolean email, boolean sms, boolean push, boolean urgent) {}
public void sendNotification(String userId, String message, NotificationOptions options) {
    if (options.email())  sendEmail(userId, message);
    if (options.sms())    sendSMS(userId, message);
    if (options.push())   sendPush(userId, message);
}
// Call site is now readable:
var opts = new NotificationOptions(true, false, true, false);
sendNotification(uid, msg, opts);

items.q:
6. What is the output?
static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = temp;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] nums = {10, 20, 30, 40};
    swap(nums, 0, 3);
    System.out.println(nums[0] + " " + nums[3]);
}

items.a:
Java doesn't have output parameters, but you can achieve multiple return values by: (1) Returning a record: record MinMax(int min, int max) {} then public MinMax findMinMax(int[] arr) — cleanest approach in Java 16+. (2) Returning a List or Map. (3) Passing a mutable container object and modifying its fields. Example with record:
record Stats(double mean, double stdDev) {}
public Stats compute(double[] data) {
    double mean = Arrays.stream(data).average().orElse(0);
    double variance = Arrays.stream(data).map(x -> (x-mean)*(x-mean)).average().orElse(0);
    return new Stats(mean, Math.sqrt(variance));
}
// Call site:
Stats s = compute(data);
System.out.println(s.mean() + " " + s.stdDev());

